node_modules/

# ── What belongs in git, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The rule: a fresh clone must be able to run `apply-migration` and `validate`.
# It cannot re-run the whole pipeline — the source documents live in ../_inbox
# and are not part of this repo — so the two files those steps READ are tracked
# even though they are generated:
#
#   audit/harvested-bound.js  2.1 MB  the lecturer's questions, images bound in.
#                                     apply-migration.mjs imports it, so without
#                                     it pack.js cannot be regenerated at all.
#   audit/corpus.json         2.4 MB  every quote-checkable location in the course
#                                     material. validate.mjs G3 reads it.
#
# Everything below is regenerable ONLY from documents this repo does not hold, so
# committing it would buy nothing but weight. If the pipeline ever has to run
# end-to-end again, those inputs come back from ../_inbox and `node audit/run-all.mjs`
# rebuilds these.

# (a `#` only opens a comment at the START of a line — a trailing one becomes part
# of the pattern, which is how the first version of this file ignored nothing.)

# 50 MB of base64 quiz screenshots
audit/question-images.json
# derived from question-images.json
audit/images-optimised.json
# sidecar for build-corpus, superseded by corpus.json
audit/pdf-units.json

# a worklist, regenerated on demand by `apply-migration.mjs --emit-missing`.
# Committing it would leave a stale list of cards that already have explanations.
audit/missing-explanations.json

# apply-migration.mjs writes both of these on every run. pack.js is the artifact.
pack.new.js
pack.backup.js

# the tripwire fixture's own validation report — a description of a deliberately
# broken pack, which must never be mistaken for the real one.
audit/validate-report.fixture.json
